  "body": "## Planning screen: certified decoder copy budgets for the plane-port coverage law (no new \u03ba)\n\n**Card:** `coverage-law-change` (recursive-interfaces track, seed `fba49a70\u2026`), scope refined. [CLAIM](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1587#discussioncomment-18921569). Base `0b1cccf8`. Adds only `research/plane-port-copy-budgets/` (stdlib, about 17 s).\n\nctoth's [FINDING in 1587](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1587#discussioncomment-18914510) shows a plane-port contract leaves **two** unpaid coordinates per family. Its first-order break-even is a decoder copy charge C < 1472/9 \u2248 163.6 per call, with the word unchanged and on the complex side only. This package prices it exactly on **both** suppliers. It imports the predecessors' engines unchanged and hash-pinned: complex `complex-local-word-ceiling/ceiling.py`, and bit `bit-revision3-levers-seed03/levers.py` with the PR589 word.\n\n### Results\nLargest per-call copy charge, as sharp integers (k passes, k + 1 fails, both certified). Each range runs from word unchanged (pessimistic) to the data shrink taken as rank-1 removals (favorable). Bit is at cap 45.\n\n| side | copy rank | tie incumbent ceiling | +10% \u03ba | a = 1/99 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| complex | 1 | 162\u2013164 | 159\u2013160 | none |\n| complex | 8 | 160 | 152\u2013160 | none |\n| bit | 1 | 160\u2013162 | 162\u2013164 | 11\u201320 |\n| bit | 8 | 160 | 160 | 8\u201316 |\n\n- **The first-order 163.6 is confirmed to within 4 units on both sides.** The data shrink (8 \u2192 7 complex, 7 \u2192 6 bit) is worth at most 2 units at the tie. Each unit of charge near 160 is worth about 3% \u03ba.\n- **On the complex side, plane ports alone cannot reach a = 1/99, even with a free decoder.** The C = 0 ceiling is 0.009410\u20130.009821. The rest of the complex word has to change too.\n- **On the bit side, 1/99 needs C \u2264 7\u201322.** ctoth's naive realization (112 rank-2 queries, C = 224) fails every target.\n- **Scope:** a fail rules out every construction under the premises; a pass is only a necessary target. The relaxation assumes ideal idle packing, the shipped non-data children kept, one copy rank and the predecessors' caps. The bit relaxation already passes +10% with the shipped word: its ceiling isn't tight, and PR1791 measures the actual histogram.\n\n### Checks\n- `verify.py` reproduces both incumbent ceilings (complex 0.001690054, bit 0.001955918756) and the per-call masses (complex idle 1646, bit local 5129).\n- Every budget's sharpness is checked, plus line-law controls.\n- `RESULT.json` matches byte for byte. The script refuses `-O`.\n- It passes on 3.10 and 3.12. `research_package.py --check-only` and the registered 3.12 replay pass, and the 42 research unit tests pass.\n- An independent float spot-check of the complex tie (162 | 163 and 164 | 165) agrees.\n\n### Not established\n- Any plane-port decoder or decoder floor (@philippeVV's halves in 1587).\n- The plane-port bit template, admission, hosting and precision.\n- ctoth's small-instance exactness check was not rerun.\n\n### Open next questions\nBoth are in the README and TASK.json:\n1. Is there a plane-port decoder with C \u2264 160 per call?\n2. Which complex word change, combined with plane ports, reaches 1/99?\n\n### Credit\n- ctoth's 1587 team: the plane-port contract, the break-even and the naive realization.\n- Douglas Colkitt: the complex ceiling.\n- The `bit-revision3-levers-seed03` authors: the bit model.\n- PR1012: the coverage identity.\n- @philippeVV: the decoder halves.\n\nClaude Code (Claude Opus 5.5) for JosieElliston, one donated session, no subagents, no independent human review. Apache-2.0.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
